WEBVTT

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.930
This is "Frontline."

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:44.550
are seeing is rare and controversial. A few years ago, it was one of the most common operations

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:50.480
performed on more than a million women. Today in America, only a handful of surgeons still

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:58.040
offer it as part of a clinical study. The silicone breast implant, a bag filled with silicone

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:00.000
implanted to augment or reconnect.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:00.800
struck breasts

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.380
has become the center

00:01:02.380 --> 00:01:03.660
of a bitter controversy

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.100
involving nearly

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:05.860
half a million

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:06.740
American women.

00:01:10.510 --> 00:01:16.360
Is this a valuable

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:17.380
medical device

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.900
or one of the greatest

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:19.900
health disasters

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.640
in history?

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:29.190
33 years ago

00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:30.000
when this woman

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.560
Timmy Jean Lindsey had the first ever silicone breast implants it seemed like a

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:41.270
good idea in March 1962 Timmy Jean then 28 years old went into a Texas hospital

00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.890
to have some tattoos removed when two plastic surgeons came to her with a

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:52.650
question they asked me would I like to have breast implants what they were gonna

00:01:52.650 --> 00:02:00.000
do would be enlarge my breast and you know after having six kids wasn't that

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:02.390
flat. It was just, they sag.

00:02:03.030 --> 00:02:04.080
Hold on, Mary.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.540
Of course I said yes.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:07.580
Almost immediately,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:08.860
she noticed an effect.

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.120
Well, it was about six weeks

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.150
surgery, and we were going to go out

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.150
to a club, so we walked into

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:18.290
this bar, and all the men

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.090
just went, wow, you know, and

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:22.290
whistle, and wolf calls

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.390
and everything, so

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.500
I thought, kind of went to my head, you know,

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:27.420
I thought, wow.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.890
But the breast

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.500
Standing out and enlarged made me my waist looks smaller and everything

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:44.170
Accentuated the waist it kind of built my self-esteem or my confidence or something like that because really I thought

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.820
After having six children that no one just probably never would attract any man or anything

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.090
But it really did the trick

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.000
In the next three decades many thousands of women followed into

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.140
Timmy Jean's footsteps.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.060
For a few thousand dollars,

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.780
women could change their shape

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:05.680
from the way they were

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.220
to the way they wanted to be.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.890
In Sunbelt states like Texas,

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:11.830
as many as one woman in 50

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:13.830
had silicone breast implant surgery.

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:22.580
There was another group of women

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.340
for whom the new prosthesis

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.600
offered a lifeline.

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.000
In 1977, when Gladys Lass

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:31.770
discovered she had lumps in her breast

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.950
and would have to undergo a radical mastectomy,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:35.190
she was devastated.

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.680
Then doctors proposed reconstructing her breasts with silicone,

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.260
offering her a possibility of being whole again.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:45.790
I had to wait a month after the mastectomies

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:46.630
to have reconstruction.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.460
So I knew what I looked like without breasts.

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:53.250
And so then a month later, once again,

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:54.990
I had beautiful breasts.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:56.420
They were soft.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:03:57.500
They were supple.

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.000
It was just impossible.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.200
possible to tell that they were not real breast.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.800
The surgeon, he told me that they were absolutely harmless

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.780
and that they would last me a lifetime.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:12.910
He said, matter of fact, Gladys, he said,

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:15.830
if you live to be 80 and die, all your friends

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.350
are going to be very envious because when you're

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:19.030
in the casket, you may be an old woman,

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:21.760
but you'll have beautiful breasts.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.070
So that sort of has stayed with me.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.010
My legs are going berserk all day.

00:04:27.010 --> 00:04:30.000
Gladys was one of many thousands of women with breast cancer

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:34.640
fibrocystic disease who following radical mastectomies had their breasts

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:44.860
reconstructed with silicone implants in the 70s and 80s. For 13 years Gladys was

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:49.110
happy with her implants but then she began to experience a litany of bizarre

00:04:49.110 --> 00:05:00.000
health problems. I started having problems probably around 1990. My shoulders would freeze up. Had to have my shoulder

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.340
injected and then I had to take physical therapy and then I would go three or

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:08.040
four months and the first thing you know they would start freezing up again and

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:14.220
so this kept happening and then I would have stinging sensation in my face that

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:19.410
would come like someone just drew a line right here just exact nowhere else and

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:23.790
it would sting sting sting like bees were stinging me and that would come and

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:30.000
go I had double vision the tip of my tongue would go numb

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:36.280
like I'd been to a dentist I could not remember things and I would drop things

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.320
or I'd knock things over I would have terrible spasms in my legs they would

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:46.310
just drop under me and my arches would curl up and my husband would have to

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.030
hold my feet on the floor in the evenings a lot I saw a lot of these

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:55.030
things before she would realize them I didn't know what was causing them and I

00:05:55.030 --> 00:06:00.000
knew that there was something wrong and when she would go to sleep on the couch

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:07.940
couch right after supper and sleep time to go to bed that was not my wife that was this person

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:13.900
that had a big problem. Gladys wasn't the only woman with breast implants having health problems

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:19.400
as Connie Chung told millions of viewers later that year. Most of us know little about breast

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:24.090
implants we've seen the ads we've heard the rumors about which celebrities have them and which don't

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:30.000
but we don't know anything about the dangers. The program told viewers that many women

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:34.320
with silicone implants were reporting patterns of illness from breast cancer

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.510
to autoimmune diseases like rheumatoid arthritis since roughly 1 million women

00:06:39.510 --> 00:06:49.940
had implants a huge public health crisis might be looming the celebrities had

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:54.560
silicone breast implants and some of them like Jenny Jones had also experienced

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.600
health problems they used their television shows to warn other women of

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.000
the dangers

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:06.400
fibromyalgia, chronic fatigue syndrome. I've been tested for scleroderma, lupus, rheumatoid

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.040
arthritis. I'm 25. I taught aerobics and I can hardly get around. I had my first heart

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:21.040
attack at 44. I'm on heart medication. I have undifferentiated lupus, Sjogren's, arthritic

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:30.000
condition. I'm too young. What kind of implants? I had the silicone breast implant. Raise your

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:36.320
had problems that's just about everybody as the media devoured the story fear

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.980
spread among American women with implants a lot of women have had them

00:07:39.980 --> 00:07:44.700
removed when they wondered would they get ill

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.000
Dow Corning the largest manufacturer of breast implants had heard some of these

00:07:51.000 --> 00:07:56.750
complaints before in the 1980s a few women had claimed that the silicone

00:07:56.750 --> 00:08:00.000
contained in the bags had leaked out damaging the immune system

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.720
and causing it to attack their own tissues which led in turn to autoimmune

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:12.900
diseases like rheumatoid arthritis some like Marion Hopkins had even taken the

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:19.110
company to court in 1991 she sued Dow Corning arguing that silicone leaking

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:24.750
from a ruptured implant caused her autoimmune disease the case was based on

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:28.470
hundreds of pages of internal memos and reports that her lawyers obtained from

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.000
Dow Corning suggesting

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:33.600
that the company had been aware of problems with the devices but had

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:38.640
continued to market them anyway. Dow Corning memo: we are hearing complaints

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.900
from the field. The general claim is that the units bleed profusely after they've

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.920
been flexed vigorously. Dow Corning memo: I have proposed again and again that we

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:52.000
must begin an in-depth study of our gel envelope and bleed phenomenon. Time is

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.680
going to run out on us if we do not get underway. Memo to Salesforce: the new

00:08:55.680 --> 00:09:00.000
implants have a tendency to appear oily after being manipulated. Wash with

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.220
soap and water in nearest washroom dry with hand towel corning memo to put a

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.240
questionable lot of implants on the market is inexcusable I don't know who's

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:11.780
responsible for this decision but it has to rank right up there with the Pinto

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:18.970
gas tank I will tell you during my trial I was just absolutely appalled by the

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:26.860
information that I heard I felt that the manufacturer had a social moral and

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:30.000
ethical responsibility to the consumers

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:34.770
and that this product should not be on the market.

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:36.160
- The jury agreed.

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:39.020
In December 1991, they awarded Marion Hopkins

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:41.820
a stunning $7.3 million,

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:47.880
claiming Dow Corning was guilty of negligence and fraud.

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:53.310
Hopkins' victory served as an inspiration for other women.

00:09:53.310 --> 00:09:56.900
All this activity had caught the attention of the FDA,

00:09:56.900 --> 00:09:58.450
which after years of doing little,

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:00.000
had finally gotten around to

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.700
asking breast implant manufacturers for comprehensive safety data.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:13.340
Commissioner David Kessler, who was appointed in 1990, felt that the FDA had let women down.

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:17.360
Kessler was concerned by a series of case reports by physicians suggesting a possible

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.550
association between breast implants and autoimmune diseases.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.850
Then he received the package of Dow Corning documents from Marion Hopkins' lawyer, and

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:30.000
he was disturbed by what he read.

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:38.630
whole set of internal Dow documents raised concerns. They were reporting

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:43.670
areas of thinness and and thickness to the outer envelope. They were talking

00:10:43.670 --> 00:10:48.900
about increasing bleed rates. They were saying that there were problems when the

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:53.100
devices were flexed that they would bleed profusely or that they would rupture.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:59.450
And when I saw those quality assurance problems and I saw that those devices had

00:10:59.450 --> 00:11:00.000
been implanted

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.650
My first concern was basically to say timeout.

00:11:03.650 --> 00:11:06.470
We need to get a handle on this.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:09.860
We need to get certain questions answered.

00:11:09.860 --> 00:11:14.510
I can't continue to allow this device to be implanted in women

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:17.510
until we have the answers to those questions.

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:22.680
On January 6, 1992, Gessler made a dramatic announcement

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.690
that caught the attention of the nation.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:30.000
I am asking that physicians cease using silicone gel implants.

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:37.090
physicians our first obligation is to do no harm we do not know exactly what

00:11:37.090 --> 00:11:41.320
material I don't think Kessler could possibly have foreseen the consequences

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:48.140
of his action walk in my shoes you want to study study me he said look I'm gonna

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:53.670
remove breast implants from the market I know that one to two million of you

00:11:53.670 --> 00:12:00.000
already have them not to worry don't get them taken out nevertheless I'm

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.580
removing them from the market so that other women can't get them.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:09.500
And I think women who had breast implants quite rightly were terrified by this.

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:13.740
And there were women who were so frightened that they did desperate things.

00:12:13.740 --> 00:12:19.320
There were a few women who tried to carve out their own implants with razor blades

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:23.400
because they couldn't afford the surgeon's fee to have them removed.

00:12:23.400 --> 00:12:29.670
This is a measure of the fear, the alarm, the desperation that his action caused.

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:37.680
Dow Corning, the largest implant manufacturer, found itself under attack from an outraged

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:38.980
media.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:42.400
Let me read a couple of the particularly disturbing quotes.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:46.160
One salesman said putting these implants on the market was, quote, inexcusable and had

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:49.560
to rank right up there with the Pinto gas tank.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:55.780
An internal document instructed salesmen to wash off the oily film off the outside of

00:12:55.780 --> 00:13:00.000
the implants, ostensibly, I'm assuming, so doctors wouldn't realize.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.200
that they, in fact, could leak.

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:04.880
You said that this is a PR nightmare,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.420
but isn't it much more than that?

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.720
Doesn't this show some sort of unethical behavior

00:13:09.720 --> 00:13:10.480
by your company?

00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:11.540
Not at all, not at all.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:13.360
Both those documents are very interesting.

00:13:13.540 --> 00:13:16.240
Both of them related to the complaint of a salesman

00:13:16.240 --> 00:13:17.660
back to his sales manager,

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:21.840
and they had to do with the appearance of the product, okay?

00:13:22.140 --> 00:13:25.620
It was not an issue that involved the safety of the product.

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:27.240
It had to do with the appearance.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.440
Stop the lies and get the facts!

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:35.110
Kessler's moratorium had turned the concerns of individual women into a political movement.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:43.650
I am appalled that the American public has not called for criminal charges against these main doctors.

00:13:46.710 --> 00:13:50.070
Thousands of women were frightened, angry, and looking for help.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:55.810
And there was no shortage of people ready to help them.

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:59.960
Like the legendary Texas attorneys John O'Quinn and Rick Laminack,

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:02.780
who some called the "Kings of Tort."

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:08.480
There were thousands and thousands, tens of thousands, of women who reported being ill.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:15.780
And so only common sense tells you with that many women that are ill, something's wrong with this product.

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:19.480
And those women, O'Quinn argued, needed expert help.

00:14:19.480 --> 00:14:26.340
These women cannot possibly get justice without highly experienced, capable lawyers

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:30.000
who are actually willing to put up the money to fight these companies.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:33.790
To do one of these cases, you've got to spend probably a million dollars

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:37.170
to get knowledgeable enough to present it.

00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:41.240
Most lawyers cannot afford to spend a million dollars to take a case to court.

00:14:43.460 --> 00:14:46.060
O'Quinn could.

00:14:46.620 --> 00:14:50.420
Later that year, he convinced a jury to find for his client Pamela Johnson

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:53.680
against implant manufacturer Bristol-Myers Squibb,

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:55.300
awarding her $25 million.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:15:00.000
With such huge sums,

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:06.280
A flood of women came forward asking O'Quinn and Laminak to sue manufacturers on their behalf.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:18.570
As the number of clients grew, O'Quinn and Laminak began holding regular meetings to brief clients and boost morale in what, for most of them, was going to be a long and bitter fight with corporate America.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:30.000
They are so scared of Rick Laminak and John O'Quinn that they put us on the front cover of their most famous magazine, Fortune.

00:15:30.220 --> 00:15:36.690
the executives of Fortune 500 read. And of course the point of the article is to demonize us,

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:42.420
so they call us the lawyers for hell. We're the lawyers that give them hell.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:58.520
Make no mistake, we are engaged in a holy war.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:07.640
we're gonna win this fight we're gonna win it because we're right and we're gonna win it because

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:15.130
like no case i've ever worked on this is the champion of all of them i've got the greatest

00:16:15.130 --> 00:16:23.540
clients who ever lived and if we're going to beat the dow machine and they got a lot of health 3m is

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:30.000
not a small squirt itself north bristol and baxter together these companies are worth in excess of 50 billion

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:40.310
It would be only because of a united effort, and your voice, your voice saying, "No, we're

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:42.520
not going to take this."

00:16:42.520 --> 00:17:00.000
All over the country, cases were filed, fueled by lawyers' advertisements, media publicity

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:02.610
and reports of enormous verdicts.

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:06.780
Women came forward, first dozens, then hundreds, then thousands,

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:11.360
all wishing to pursue cases against Dow Corning and the other manufacturers.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:16.350
Soon it was clear that this would become the largest mass toward in history,

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:20.850
eclipsing litigation for asbestos, Delcon Shield, and Agent Orange.

00:17:21.110 --> 00:17:25.360
There were so many cases that they threatened to choke the legal system.

00:17:27.080 --> 00:17:30.000
The growing litigation crisis quickly attracted the attention

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.020
of specialists in mass tort litigation

00:17:32.020 --> 00:17:34.300
like the Cincinnati lawyer Stan Chesley.

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:35.500
Where's Ginny Ellsberg?

00:17:36.490 --> 00:17:37.990
When's he coming back?

00:17:38.500 --> 00:17:40.950
Chesley realized that something had to be done,

00:17:40.950 --> 00:17:42.890
and quickly, otherwise there would be

00:17:42.890 --> 00:17:44.930
total chaos, and women might not

00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:47.640
get anything. The average federal

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:49.860
judge, or the average state judge,

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.740
is not going to spend the rest

00:17:51.740 --> 00:17:53.020
of their judicial career

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:56.140
trying only breast implant cases.

00:17:56.710 --> 00:17:58.080
Oh, hi. I'm

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.000
Judge Smith, and from now on, I'm

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:06.230
I'm 40 years old, and from now on until I retire, I will try nothing but breast implant cases.

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:12.940
-Chesley saw the opportunity to create a massive class action by amalgamating thousands

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:16.760
of women into a class and representing them all at once.

00:18:16.760 --> 00:18:20.250
Their chances of getting compensation were much better.

00:18:20.250 --> 00:18:26.610
-The class action was a means to put some order in this, because the key was to build

00:18:26.610 --> 00:18:30.000
a united front and go after the defendants.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.860
everybody was going off on their own, one might sue Dow Corning, one might sue 3M. Somebody may

00:18:35.860 --> 00:18:41.270
wanted to go after the raw manufacturer, the raw product manufacturer. There were certain defendants

00:18:41.270 --> 00:18:48.090
that we already knew were bankrupt or almost bankrupt. And so my view about the class action

00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:55.800
was more a procedural case management tool. It was a means to put some order in this.

00:18:57.160 --> 00:19:00.000
But lawyers like O'Quinn and Laminak thought Chesley was trying

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.880
to take control of perhaps the most lucrative litigation of the century.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:09.380
They urged their clients not to join Chesley, who had a reputation for settling quick,

00:19:09.800 --> 00:19:13.190
but stick with them and perhaps get multi-million dollar settlements.

